WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.130
This week would have been the 80th birthday

2
00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:07.740
of iconic martial arts film star, Bruce Lee.

3
00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:11.380
He died unexpectedly at age 32, but his legacy

4
00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:15.180
and his work endure and much in part

5
00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:16.970
to the remarkable efforts

6
00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:19.320
of his daughter Shannon Lee

7
00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:21.250
and in her new book,

8
00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:25.560
Be Water My Friend, The Teachings of Bruce Lee,

9
00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:27.350
is available now.

10
00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:29.190
I've been reading it, it's incredible,

11
00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:32.120
Shannon, thank you so much for joining us.

12
00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:33.730
(audience clapping)

13
00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:34.677
Thank you, thank you.

14
00:00:34.677 --> 00:00:37.727
You know, there was a sentence that really spoke to me.

15
00:00:37.727 --> 00:00:41.027
"How does one honor the plain fact of their DNA,

16
00:00:41.027 --> 00:00:43.147
"while at the same time understanding

17
00:00:43.147 --> 00:00:44.757
"that it doesn't mean anything

18
00:00:44.757 --> 00:00:46.797
"about one's own soul, or does it?

19
00:00:46.797 --> 00:00:48.997
"Throw in my decision to spend a good portion

20
00:00:48.997 --> 00:00:51.757
"of my life protecting and promoting the legacy

21
00:00:51.757 --> 00:00:55.517
"of one of the humans who gifted me with this life

22
00:00:55.517 --> 00:00:58.247
"and who has meant so much to me".

23
00:01:00.664 --> 00:01:02.880
I really appreciated reading that,

24
00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:07.420
as someone who is very loyal,

25
00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:12.240
very inspired by my own family,

26
00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.960
and really wanna carry on their legacy

27
00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:18.220
and you've done such an amazing job

28
00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:22.070
carrying the torch for someone who is

29
00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:24.500
so beloved,

30
00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:26.160
idolized.

31
00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:30.440
He is such an important figure, to so many of us.

32
00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:31.273
Well, thank you.

33
00:01:31.273 --> 00:01:34.720
And by the way, thank you for expressing that

34
00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:37.640
and pulling that section out of the book,

35
00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:39.140
so thank you.
So glad.

36
00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:43.091
I just love your dad so much Shannon.

37
00:01:43.091 --> 00:01:43.924
(laughing)

38
00:01:43.924 --> 00:01:46.230
I watched all of his movies, I was obsessed

39
00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:50.700
and when I got to go to make Charlie's Angels,

40
00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:52.910
I showed them a clip reel I made,

41
00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:56.360
like a visual tape of scene-to-scene footage

42
00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:59.930
and one of my big things was Bruce Lee.

43
00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:02.680
And what's it like to have the coolest

44
00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:05.690
most appealing,

45
00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:08.633
capable dad?

46
00:02:09.715 --> 00:02:11.940
(laughing)

47
00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:16.190
I mean, on the one hand, it's amazing, right?

48
00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:19.140
You're like yeah, that's my dad.

49
00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:20.200
You know?

50
00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:21.410
But on the other hand too,

51
00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:22.890
like growing up sometimes

52
00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:26.320
it can feel a little like pressure,

53
00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:27.950
but it's pretty awesome, I mean,

54
00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:30.000 line:15%
when I was a kid, I used to say to people

55
00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:33.070 line:15%
when people would say, "Oh, my dad can beat up your dad"

56
00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:36.247 line:15%
It'd be like, "no but really my dad can,

57
00:02:36.247 --> 00:02:40.042 line:15%
"my dad could beat up your dad for sure, 100%"

58
00:02:40.042 --> 00:02:41.460 line:15%
(laughing)

59
00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.157
You're like, "my dad can beat up your dad.

60
00:02:43.157 --> 00:02:47.073
"all of his friends in this entire town, in one fell swoop"

61
00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:49.450
Exactly.

62
00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:52.900
Exactly, no but I think what you speak to is really amazing,

63
00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:55.243
his energy,

64
00:02:56.108 --> 00:02:56.941
his dynamism,

65
00:02:56.941 --> 00:03:00.043
his power, his essence,

66
00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:04.150
and how comfortable he was in his skin

67
00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:08.540
and the way he just occupied space fully,

68
00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:11.480
is phenomenal.

69
00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:15.050
It was really interesting to me to understand

70
00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:18.260
when, please tell me if I'm getting this right,

71
00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:23.260
his master was explaining the water metaphor.

72
00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:26.770
I mean, the way he said it is water can flow

73
00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:29.240
or it can crash, right?

74
00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:31.500
Like water is the softest,

75
00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:36.500
most gentle life-giving pliable, beautiful thing,

76
00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:39.550
but it's also can be a tsunami.

77
00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:44.010
And I mean, this is like a lifelong practice, right?

78
00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:45.380
Like how to find balance

79
00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:48.160
and what your particular balance is,

80
00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:51.120
and what's called for in a particular situation.

81
00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:53.670
There's never just one solution,

82
00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:56.220
never just one right answer,

83
00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:57.900
you have to be present to it.

84
00:03:57.900 --> 00:03:59.920
And I think that's a lot like parenting,

85
00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:02.150
I have a toolbox for my kids

86
00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:04.650
and I try to practice a few different things,

87
00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:06.570
so they're familiar and consistent,

88
00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:09.440
but that tool that worked yesterday might not work today,

89
00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:11.610
so I better have a backup plan.

90
00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:13.980
And it's funny too, 'cause my mom

91
00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:16.370
was one of those moms who was like,

92
00:04:16.370 --> 00:04:18.530
I'm just gonna try a bunch of stuff with my kids,

93
00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:21.620
you know, at the tap, jazz, piano, ballet.

94
00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:24.090
And I can't even tell you how like crappy

95
00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:26.740
I felt in a ballet class, I didn't have the body,

96
00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:28.310
I wasn't that type,

97
00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:31.530
and I finally found karate

98
00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:34.310
and all of a sudden I was like,

99
00:04:34.310 --> 00:04:35.470
these are my people.

100
00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:36.920
You want me to punch something?

101
00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:40.350
I have so much anger that I'm supposed to not let out

102
00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:43.970
but I have it in me, but it was the first time in my life,

103
00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:46.330
I remember finding a place,

104
00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:48.850
that I really felt like physically,

105
00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:52.300
emotionally and character wise,

106
00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:54.490
I just felt finally at peace.

107
00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:55.323
Totally.

108
00:04:55.323 --> 00:05:00.280
It gives you this sense of internal strength I think,

109
00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:02.920
which is like the most amazing part of it.

110
00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:04.300
More than the external strength,

111
00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:07.303
it's the internal strength that I love so much.

112
00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:13.610
Before we go, I also found a detail that I really loved,

113
00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:16.410
which is that the San Francisco Giants

114
00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:21.410
hold an annual Bruce Lee night.

115
00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:22.650
Yep.

116
00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:24.226
That is so cool

117
00:05:24.226 --> 00:05:25.810
and as soon as it's safe

118
00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:27.610
I feel like I need to go to this,

119
00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:30.670
because I feel like I will be looking around and going,

120
00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:33.101
I found my people again.

121
00:05:33.101 --> 00:05:34.150
(laughing)

122
00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:35.600
Well, you're definitely invited.

123
00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:38.760
We go every year, except of course for this past year

124
00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:42.200
and you know, especially this year as you said,

125
00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:43.780
it's going to be his 80th birthday.

126
00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:45.380
He was born in San Francisco,

127
00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:47.600
so it's his hometown.

128
00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:50.240
So anytime you wanna come, let's do it.

129
00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:53.560
Well, perhaps next year we can light the torch

130
00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:56.030
for your incredible father,

131
00:05:56.030 --> 00:05:58.070
who made the most important mark

132
00:05:58.070 --> 00:06:00.500
for so many of us, in this world.

133
00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:02.550
I'm so amazed at how beautifully

134
00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:04.290
you're upholding his legacy,

135
00:06:04.290 --> 00:06:06.840
while speaking so many interesting,

136
00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:09.020
internal truths about yourself.

137
00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:11.100
Oh my gosh, everything that you said

138
00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:13.690
like you feel this pull this sort

139
00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:17.610
of like spiritual alliance to your heritage

140
00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:20.563
that is and isn't,

141
00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:24.744
at the same time,

142
00:06:24.744 --> 00:06:26.730
it just resonates so much with me

143
00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:28.220
and so I'm really grateful,

144
00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:30.040
to be able to have a conversation with you

145
00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:30.873
and thank you.

146
00:06:30.873 --> 00:06:32.830
Thank you so much, Shannon Lee.

147
